\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The threshold trade-off, on the stored $T=400$ clean event of the canonical campaign: (a) segment efficiency and (b) segment false rate as the threshold $\tau $ sweeps, for the classical solver, the 1BQF and the QSVT (degree 40, base $A$; quantum amplitudes on the classical signal scale). The dashed verticals are each solver's efficiency-first working point (the lowest threshold keeping 99\% of its true segments). The 1BQF's efficiency steps down at its reshaped endpoint band near $\tau \approx 0.28$, so its working point sits below that band, while the QSVT holds both high efficiency and a $\sim 10^{-2}$ false rate over the whole usable range.\relax }}{14}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:threshold}{{6}{14}{The threshold trade-off, on the stored $T=400$ clean event of the canonical campaign: (a) segment efficiency and (b) segment false rate as the threshold $\tau $ sweeps, for the classical solver, the 1BQF and the QSVT (degree 40, base $A$; quantum amplitudes on the classical signal scale). The dashed verticals are each solver's efficiency-first working point (the lowest threshold keeping 99\% of its true segments). The 1BQF's efficiency steps down at its reshaped endpoint band near $\tau \approx 0.28$, so its working point sits below that band, while the QSVT holds both high efficiency and a $\sim 10^{-2}$ false rate over the whole usable range.\relax }{figure.caption.7}{}}
