\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The chain ladder, on the same analytic footing as figure\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {fig:atlas} ($\gamma =3$, $\delta =1$, $s=4$, no noise, the filtered amplitudes on the classical scale by the same global factor). Classical outer activation climbs eq.\nobreakspace  {}\textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:ladder}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} toward $0.366$; the 1BQF pushes the outer level of a five-hit track down to $0.246$ and the middles up to $0.498$, and annihilates the one-segment stub outright, since a lone segment sits exactly on the notch. The marked levels are what a threshold has to clear to keep every segment of a complete track, and the reason the earlier fixed cut at $0.35$ lost the filtered chain's endpoints.\relax }}{15}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ladder}{{7}{15}{The chain ladder, on the same analytic footing as figure~\ref {fig:atlas} ($\gamma =3$, $\delta =1$, $s=4$, no noise, the filtered amplitudes on the classical scale by the same global factor). Classical outer activation climbs eq.~\eqref {eq:ladder} toward $0.366$; the 1BQF pushes the outer level of a five-hit track down to $0.246$ and the middles up to $0.498$, and annihilates the one-segment stub outright, since a lone segment sits exactly on the notch. The marked levels are what a threshold has to clear to keep every segment of a complete track, and the reason the earlier fixed cut at $0.35$ lost the filtered chain's endpoints.\relax }{figure.caption.8}{}}
